= Metadata =

== Overview ==
'''Metadata''' is structured information about resources that supports description, discovery, management, preservation, interpretation, rights, provenance or use.

A common shorthand is "data about data", but this is incomplete.

Metadata can describe:
* books;
* images;
* people;
* physical objects;
* datasets;
* software;
* services;
* scientific samples;
* concepts.

The resource being described does not have to be "data."

== Descriptive metadata ==
'''Descriptive metadata''' helps identify and discover a resource.

Examples:
* title;
* creator;
* subject;
* abstract;
* keywords;
* language.

Library catalogs are classic metadata systems.

== Structural metadata ==
'''Structural metadata''' describes how components fit together.

Examples:
* page order in a digitized book;
* chapters in a document;
* files in a dataset package;
* relationships among media tracks.

It helps systems reconstruct compound objects.

== Administrative metadata ==
Administrative metadata supports management.

Examples:
* owner;
* access permissions;
* license;
* file format;
* creation date;
* retention policy.

It may include technical, rights and preservation information.

== Preservation metadata ==
Preservation metadata records information needed to keep resources usable over time.

It can include:
* file formats;
* fixity hashes;
* migrations;
* preservation actions;
* hardware/software dependencies;
* custody history.

This overlaps strongly with [[Provenance|provenance]].

== Provenance metadata ==
Provenance metadata records how a resource originated and changed.

Examples:
* source dataset;
* processing activity;
* responsible agent;
* transformation;
* version.

W3C PROV is one formal model for provenance metadata.

== Metadata schema ==
A metadata schema or vocabulary defines which properties are available and what they mean.

Dublin Core includes widely reused terms such as:
* title;
* creator;
* subject;
* date;
* identifier;
* rights.

The metadata schema is different from a particular metadata record.

== Embedded and external metadata ==
Metadata can be embedded in the resource.

Examples:
* EXIF in an image;
* document properties;
* HTML metadata.

Or it can be stored externally:
* catalog record;
* sidecar file;
* database row;
* RDF graph.

Each approach has persistence and synchronization trade-offs.

== Machine-readable metadata ==
Machine-readable metadata uses structured formats and identifiers that software can process reliably.

Examples include:
* JSON;
* XML;
* RDF;
* schema.org markup.

Machine-readable does not mean semantically correct.

The values and vocabulary still need interpretation and quality control.

== Metadata quality ==
Metadata can be:
* missing;
* stale;
* inconsistent;
* duplicated;
* incorrectly mapped;
* overly broad.

Useful metadata quality dimensions include:
* completeness;
* accuracy;
* consistency;
* timeliness;
* provenance;
* vocabulary control.

== Metadata and schema ==
[[Schema]] defines structure.

Metadata is information that populates/describes resources using such structures.

A metadata vocabulary can itself be represented by a schema or ontology.

== Metadata and knowledge graphs ==
A [[Knowledge graph]] can treat metadata as ordinary graph assertions.

For example:

<pre>
Dataset42 creator Alice
Dataset42 license CC-BY
Dataset42 derivedFrom Dataset10
</pre>

The difference between "metadata" and "domain data" can therefore depend on perspective.

== Epistemic notes ==
Metadata is not automatically authoritative. A metadata claim such as "created by Alice" still requires provenance and can be wrong.

